{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789485424,"nonce":"pWel0FaswNcaHl4DBwm7IoAbHGg=","title":"A string box carries its hash, and a dict probe tries identity before equality","message":"Every dict lookup on a string key hashed the string (FNV over the bytes through three calls) and `d[k] = d.get(k, 0) + v` hashed it twice; the key boxes a program shares (a literal, a key read back from the dict) were hashed on every use.\n\nNow a string box is allocated with a word after its header, zero until `zb_any_hash` computes the hash and records it there (a hash that comes out zero is recorded as one); every maker of a string box lays the word down (`DynamicBox::into_raw` in the SDK, the runtime's box makers, the boxes pass for a pointer box under the string tag), and `zyntax_box_hash` / `zyntax_box_set_hash` read and write it, expanded to a load and a store by the boxes pass. A string is immutable, so the hash is good for as long as the box is.\n\nA dict probe compares the stored key's address with the one looked up before calling `zb_any_eq`: a literal key or a key read back from the dict is the same box.\n\nMeasured on the M1 Pro: alone, neither changes the tokenize counting loop measurably (a lookup is dominated by its call chain, six calls deep from `zb_dict_get_default` to `$String$equals`); they hold for the literal-key lookups of bench_records and every lookup by a key the program keeps.","files":null}]}